WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.886
On Day 24, ci.yml ran the tests once.

2
00:00:05.886 --> 00:00:08.272
That was the whole pipeline.

3
00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:10.195
A real pipeline checks more.

4
00:00:10.195 --> 00:00:14.453
And it has to be quick, because people wait for it before they merge.

5
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.123
So the rule is simple.

6
00:00:16.123 --> 00:00:19.029
Cheap checks go first, and slow ones last.

7
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.343
Lint, with ruff, takes seconds.

8
00:00:21.343 --> 00:00:22.173
Then pytest.

9
00:00:22.173 --> 00:00:25.492
Then the Docker image build, the slowest of all.

10
00:00:25.667 --> 00:00:28.196
The lab splits the work into three jobs.

11
00:00:28.196 --> 00:00:33.445
Each job runs on its own runner, and shows up on the pull request as its own check.

12
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.806
Jobs run in parallel by default.

13
00:00:35.806 --> 00:00:37.667
Nothing waits for anything.

14
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.136
Your app should work on Python 3.13 and 3.14.

15
00:00:41.136 --> 00:00:46.325
A matrix runs one job definition several times, with different values.

16
00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:48.633
So one test job becomes two.

17
00:00:48.633 --> 00:00:52.965
They show up as two checks, test (3.13) and test (3.14).

18
00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:56.056
The build job says needs, lint and test.

19
00:00:56.056 --> 00:00:57.006
Now it waits.

20
00:00:57.006 --> 00:01:00.001
It starts only after all of them succeed.

21
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.336
Open a pull request, and watch.

22
00:01:03.336 --> 00:01:06.436
Lint and both tests start at the same moment.

23
00:01:06.436 --> 00:01:08.089
Lint is done in seconds.

24
00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:11.907
Then the tests pass, and only then does build start.

25
00:01:11.907 --> 00:01:14.461
When it passes too, every check is green.

26
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:17.391
But green only helps if red stops the merge.

27
00:01:17.391 --> 00:01:20.309
On GitHub you get that with a ruleset on main.

28
00:01:20.467 --> 00:01:23.083
It lists the check names that must pass.

29
00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:24.064
Here, all four.

30
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:30.417
When you click merge, GitHub compares the checks on the pull request's newest commit with that list.

31
00:01:30.417 --> 00:01:32.344
Every name is there, and green.

32
00:01:32.344 --> 00:01:33.649
The merge is allowed.

33
00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:40.431
Now a teammate's pull request adds import json to app.py, and never uses it.

34
00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.354
Lint finds it in seconds, and turns red.

35
00:01:43.354 --> 00:01:46.245
Ruff exits with code 1, so the step fails.

36
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:47.729
Both tests still pass.

37
00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:50.991
But build is skipped, because one of its needs failed.

38
00:01:50.991 --> 00:01:54.676
You hear about the problem without waiting for the slow part.

39
00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:56.879
The ruleset sees lint failed.

40
00:01:56.879 --> 00:01:58.336
The merge is blocked.

41
00:01:58.533 --> 00:01:59.584
Here is a trap.

42
00:01:59.584 --> 00:02:02.387
What if the ruleset required only build?

43
00:02:02.533 --> 00:02:06.488
A job that was skipped because its needs failed counts as passed.

44
00:02:06.488 --> 00:02:08.861
So the broken pull request could merge.

45
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.359
That is why you require the lint and test checks too, and not only build.

46
00:02:14.733 --> 00:02:16.790
One more thing keeps CI quick.

47
00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:19.327
Every job starts on an empty machine.

48
00:02:19.327 --> 00:02:23.372
So pip install downloads every package again, on every run.

49
00:02:23.533 --> 00:02:27.399
A cache saves files from one run, and restores them in the next.

50
00:02:27.399 --> 00:02:29.755
Each entry is stored under a cache key.

51
00:02:29.933 --> 00:02:39.156
With cache: pip, setup-python builds the key from the runner's OS and CPU, the Python version, and a hash of your requirements files.

52
00:02:39.333 --> 00:02:42.985
On the first run, the store of saved caches is empty.

53
00:02:42.985 --> 00:02:44.019
No key matches.

54
00:02:44.019 --> 00:02:45.052
That is a miss.

55
00:02:45.052 --> 00:02:49.600
The log says pip cache is not found, and pip downloads everything.

56
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:53.049
A job that missed saves a new entry when it finishes.

57
00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.343
Next run, the same key exists.

58
00:02:55.343 --> 00:02:56.272
An exact hit.

59
00:02:56.272 --> 00:03:00.559
The files come back, and pip has almost nothing to download.

60
00:03:00.733 --> 00:03:03.161
Now you change requirements.txt.

61
00:03:03.161 --> 00:03:06.271
Its hash changes, so the key changes too.

62
00:03:06.271 --> 00:03:07.409
No exact match.

63
00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:10.265
But a key with the same start is there.

64
00:03:10.265 --> 00:03:12.520
Same OS, same Python, older hash.

65
00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:14.023
That is a partial hit.

66
00:03:14.023 --> 00:03:18.464
Those older files come back, and pip downloads only what changed.

67
00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.743
GitHub deletes caches nobody used for seven days.

68
00:03:21.743 --> 00:03:24.885
So treat the cache as a speed-up that can vanish.

69
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.763
Cheap checks first, and build waits with needs.

70
00:03:28.763 --> 00:03:31.538
A matrix gives one check per Python version.

71
00:03:31.538 --> 00:03:34.123
The ruleset requires every check by name.

72
00:03:34.123 --> 00:03:36.771
And the cache key decides what comes back.
